Phase 10, Deployment and MLOps. Shipping once is easy. Shipping repeatedly, safely, and with evidence is the job. Containers, pipelines, rollback plans, and monitoring turn a model into an operational system. This phase has 5 lessons. Lesson 1: Evaluate like an engineer. Evaluation is testing: fixed inputs, a declared protocol, meaningful slices, and reproducible evidence. The question for this lesson: How do you know an improvement is real?. A score is a claim. Evaluation is the cross-examination. Did the improvement survive a different split? Does it hold for the subgroup that matters? Is it larger than the noise you measured last week? Accuracy is a terrible narrator for rare events. Precision asks how often a positive prediction is right. Recall asks how many real positives you found. F1 balances them, but no single number can tell you whether the model is useful in the world. You will learn: Cross-validation estimates variation., Slice metrics reveal hidden failures., Regression tests pin known behavior.. Practice: "Create an evaluation report with overall score. Watch out for: One average metric., No high-impact slices., Unsaved predictions.. You are ready when: Freeze evaluation data., Set quality gates., Review errors.. Recommended video: "ROC and AUC. Lesson 2: Beat overfitting with L1 and L2. Regularization modifies the objective so unnecessary complexity is expensive. It reduces variance at the cost of some bias, so strength must be selected through validation. The question for this lesson: "How do I make the model learn signal. Overfitting is memorizing the practice test. The model gets better and better on data it has seen while quietly getting worse on data it has not. Regularization makes complicated answers expensive, so the model prefers simpler ones unless the evidence is strong. L1 adds the absolute value of the weights to the loss, and that tends to push some weights all the way to zero. L2 adds squared weights, shrinking everything smoothly. Neither is magic; both need a strength chosen inside cross-validation. You will learn: L1 penalizes absolute weights and can make coefficients exactly zero., L2 penalizes squared weights and smoothly shrinks correlated features., Tune regularization strength inside cross-validation.. Practice: "Compare unregularized. Watch out for: Tuning strength on the test set., Scaling outside a pipeline., Assuming L1 always chooses the truth.. You are ready when: Use a pipeline., Tune with CV., Report both train and validation.. Recommended video: Regularization Part 1: Ridge (L2) Regression. Lesson 3: Monitor and improve. Production starts a new learning loop. Watch service health, data drift, prediction distributions, delayed quality labels, and the real decision outcome. The question for this lesson: How will you know when the model stops being useful?. Your model is not a sculpture; it is a houseplant. The world changes, the inputs change, and the relationship between them changes. A model that was accurate in January can be confidently wrong by June. Monitoring is how you notice before your users do. Watch the boring service metrics, then watch the data: input drift, prediction drift, and eventually the quality labels that arrive too late to prevent the mistake but early enough to fix the next one. You will learn: Input drift and concept drift are different failures., Observability links request, model version, output, latency, and eventual outcome., Rollback and champion-challenger plans make changes safer.. Practice: "Design a dashboard for request volume. Watch out for: Monitoring uptime only., Alerting on every small change., Deploying with no rollback.. You are ready when: Define alert owners., Version artifacts., Plan rollback and retraining.. Recommended video: Data Drift vs Model Drift in Machine Learning. Lesson 4: Containerize and deploy. A container pins the runtime, not the model quality. Build a small image, pass configuration at runtime, add a health check, and deploy to one real host. The question for this lesson: Can you reproduce the service outside your laptop?. Containers solve the “works on my machine” problem by shipping the runtime with the code. They do not solve data drift, model versioning, or bad configuration. The image is one layer of reproducibility, not the whole system. Keep the image small and the configuration external. Secrets come from the platform, not the Dockerfile. A health endpoint and a startup check tell the platform whether the process is actually ready to serve. You will learn: Pin base images and dependencies for repeatable builds., Configuration and secrets belong in runtime environment, not layers., Health and readiness checks are part of the deploy contract.. Practice: "Write a multi-stage Dockerfile for the prediction API. Watch out for: Using latest tags with no lockfile., Baking API keys or model credentials into the image., Deploying without a health check or rollback path.. You are ready when: Build from a clean checkout., Run the container locally., Verify health after deploy.. Lesson 5: CI/CD and release gates. Automate tests, builds, and deployment checks. A release gate should stop a regression before users see it, not after the incident review. The question for this lesson: How do you make a safe change on a Friday afternoon?. Continuous integration answers a simple question: does this change still build and pass its checks? Continuous delivery extends that to packaging and deployment, with the same evidence attached to every release. A model pipeline needs more than unit tests. Add schema checks, a small evaluation slice, artifact versioning, and a manual approval for production if the risk warrants it. The goal is boring, reversible releases. You will learn: CI should run the same checks locally and remotely., Evaluation gates belong in the release path, not a forgotten notebook., Every deploy needs an artifact version and a rollback plan.. Practice: "Add a GitHub Actions workflow that installs. Watch out for: Deploying on every push with no gate., Using unpinned actions or credentials in logs., Building an image that was never tested.. You are ready when: Run checks locally., Pin actions., Verify rollback.. A containerized service that deploys through CI and reports its own health. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
